cff-version: 1.2.0
title: Quantifying creativity analysis
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite it using the
  metadata from this file.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Anna
    family-names: Bakker
    email: anb@cswalcheren.nl
repository-code: 'https://github.com/Unitato99/Quantifying-creativity/'
abstract: >-
  Creativity is an important higher order cognitive skill in
  solving physics problems. However, it is notoriously
  difficult and time-consuming to measure. Nowadays, solving
  complex physics problems is mostly done by multiple
  research groups in parallel. In such contexts, it is
  essential that the research community as a whole generates
  creative solutions to the problem at hand, motivating the
  need for collective creativity. We apply various methods
  to quantify collective creativity to undergraduate
  students’ open-ended experimental design proposals in the
  physics subfield of soft matter.  We  compare and discuss
  the three most promising of these methods. After
  comparison, a probability-based metric of the proposal
  distribution emerges as the most valid and distinctive
  quantification of collective creativity. It is capable of
  resolving year-to-year variations in collective creativity
  that are not readily apparent from qualitative inspection
  alone. This new metric can inform evidence-based decisions
  on how lab courses may be adapted to more effectively
  stimulate student collective creativity.
